17592186124652 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124652 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124652 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124652 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Quadric Surfaces and Cylinders With Alternative Orientations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124652 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124652 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "89fbf42b-d171-5772-87ce-cf622b720963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124700 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124700 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124700 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124700 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124700 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124700 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124700 #edb/ref 1045 "Identifying Quadric Surfaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124700 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124700 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124700 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124700 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186124700 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "128c541d-1422-5228-a02e-c5d080886a64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124831 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124831 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164373" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124831 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124831 #edb/ref 1071 "Identify the surface ${z}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124831 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4c96dde-6df8-575b-bdd9-f49e9283853d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124831 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the equation of a hyperboloid of one sheet centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, a hyperboloid of one sheet centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $x$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is a hyperboloid of one sheet whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis and $a=b=c=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125536 #edb/ref 1061 31030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125536 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c326de48-b43d-56d2-9c9e-007c179f58ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125536 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125641 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125641 #edb/ref 1061 45294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125641 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c5b55480-9dcd-56e9-acf7-87ada2ec2005" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125641 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125642 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125642 #edb/ref 1061 45296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125642 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b00e77f6-434b-5bca-9612-5405fa8cbcd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125642 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125643 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125643 #edb/ref 1061 45300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125643 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "45bf287e-52f8-5fe1-a4fe-73b8c92a2ec7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125643 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125648 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125648 #edb/ref 1061 45333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125648 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7440cca3-653a-5d59-a5d4-2b047f4c7357" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125648 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125883 #edb/ref 1064 "A table showing the most common cylinders whose base curve is centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane is given below.\n\n| Surface | Equation | Image |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Elliptic Cylinder | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1$ | ![](images/2b/2b8788f027b93593a025542face33546425b057d1eb3b8e18da26e7774f6ed20.png) |\n| Parabolic Cylinder | $x^2=4py$\n\n$y^2=4px$ | ![](images/d3/d3f31aae01222e6a63e5de185c5b9f5a8af5aed8a750c6bc230e20e9fd93871a.png) |\n| Hyperbolic Cylinder | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1$\n\n$\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}=1$ | ![](images/15/159bb67600e079d9943ba6f79fcc6e580e3718f822dce50620c2bb6d2d9b76c8.png) |\n\nSimilar to quadric surfaces:\n\n- We can generate cylinders with alternative orientation axes by describing a base curve in the $xz$-or $yz$-planes. \n\nFor example, the equation of an elliptic cylinder with its axis of symmetry along the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} = 1.\n$$\n\n- We can shift the center/vertex of the base curve in the usual way." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125883 #edb/ref 1065 7843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125883 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "41c3acb2-b2af-59b4-a6bb-3883e59406f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125883 #edb/ref 1067 "A Table of Common Cylinders" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125888 #edb/ref 1064 "The most common quadric surfaces are those centered at the origin. We describe these common surfaces in the table below.\n\n| Surface | Equation | Image |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ellipsoid | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}+\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} = 1$ | ![](images/09/09dc100248a28a5518bcd8428368edefc7c3ee8d5ef0e50515cb7552ee54a376.png) |\n| Elliptic Paraboloid | $z = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/76/76e4ad8cb1e83eadae4dce865e100c9a716f9830e7f12adb367b2a9b74d1a651.png) |\n| Hyperbolic Paraboloid | $z = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/cb/cbc04d7f98e1bb7ce0d62eda71102a6abad708dfcedd7aabee8fff23281471dd.png) |\n| Hyperboloid of One Sheet | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1$ | ![](images/df/df6ef914bc78e121d75baf9f5147784d0e6e434d76800eff60dcb3c3d764477c.png) |\n| Hyperboloid of Two Sheets | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=-1$ | ![](images/08/0870b95c5ce1133d6645e3063174157e36c4354df370c05e48218111ea714838.png) |\n| Elliptic Cone | $\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/0f/0f492bf07e573ec7858977e48fa4bf81635e61520b5caa9edef14bd259184347.png) |\n\nAlso, recall the following from previous lessons:\n\n- We can generate paraboloids, hyperboloids, and cones with alternative orientation axes by permuting the variables in the corresponding equation. \n\nFor example, the equation of an elliptic cone with its vertex at $O$ and axis of symmetry along the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}.\n$$\n\n- We can shift the center/vertex/saddle point of a quadric surface to $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ using the usual coordinate transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) \\longrightarrow (x-x_0,y-y_0,z-z_0)\n$$\n\nFinally, there is a simple way to distinguish between hyperboloids with one and two sheets by simply looking at the equations:\n\n- *one* minus sign indicates hyperboloids of *one* sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1\n$$\n\n- *two* minus signs indicate hyperboloids of *two* sheets:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-}1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125888 #edb/ref 1065 7860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125888 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e806c3d0-0e3e-584e-93eb-1406ac607ad3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125888 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281807 #edb/ref 1080 "An elliptic cone whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis" #edb/ref 17592